ADDUSER(8)                     P32W Unix                      ADDUSER(8)

NAME
       adduser - add a user account

SYNOPSIS
       /etc/adduser [-p] [-u uid] [-g group] [-c comment] name

DESCRIPTION
       adduser adds the user name: it appends the line

              name::uid:gid:comment:/usr/name:/bin/sh

       to /etc/passwd, makes the home directory /usr/name, owned
       by the new user and group, mode 755, and an empty mailbox
       /usr/spool/mail/name, owned by the user, group mail, mode
       660 (see mail(1)). The account has no
       password until one is set with passwd(1): set one at once,
       since an account without a password needs none to log in.

       name is 1 to 8 characters: lower-case letters, digits and _,
       not starting with a digit. It must not exist already. The
       uid is the lowest free one from 100 up, the group staff
       (gid 10). Only root may add users.

OPTIONS
       -p     private: the home directory is mode 700.
       -u uid use this uid; it must not be taken.
       -g group
               the login group, by name from /etc/group or by
               number.
       -c comment
               the comment (full name) field; it may not hold a
               colon.

EXAMPLES
       # /etc/adduser -c Dennis dmr
       dmr: uid 101, gid 10, home /usr/dmr; set a password with:
       passwd dmr
       # passwd dmr

EXIT STATUS
       0 on success; 1 if not root, for a bad or existing name, a
       taken uid, an unknown group, or if /usr/name or /etc/passwd
       cannot be written; 2 for a usage error.

FILES
       /etc/passwd    the accounts
       /etc/group     group names
       /usr/name      the new home directory
       /usr/spool/mail/name
                      the new mailbox

SEE ALSO
       passwd(1), passwd(5), group(5), login(1), su(1), chown(1)

BUGS
       The user is not added to the member list in /etc/group, and
       the shell is always /bin/sh. There is no deluser: remove the
       line from /etc/passwd with ed(1) and the home with rm -r.

       /etc/passwd is not locked while it is written. The home is
       always under /usr.
